    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Huntsville earned its name from a long hunter who camped under a rock shelter in the mid‑1700s, honoring early settlers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Huntsville%2C_Tennessee"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Chosen as Scott County’s seat in 1849, Huntsville’s central spot and spring made it the county’s administrative heart.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11,
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Huntsville%2C_Tennessee"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "A 1906 courthouse square was built from beige sandstone, but a 1946 fire left only the First National Bank and Old Scott County Jail standing.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15,
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Huntsville%2C_Tennessee"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Lowe Mill, a former factory, now hosts 152 studios, seven galleries, a theatre, and a community garden for over 200 artists.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.downtownhuntsville.org/downtownguide"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "A preserved hardware and furniture store, now a museum, showcases local artisans’ goods inside its historic walls.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7,
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.downtownhuntsville.org/downtownguide"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Perched atop the Cumberland Plateau, Huntsville overlooks cliffs that watch the New River, about 15 miles upstream from its mouth.",
